\subsection*{Parts A-2. Calculating initial velocities}
 \begin{steps}
        \newstep[] In the following you will start the enzyme-catalyzed reaction to measure initial velocities.
                \newstep[] Find three new jumbo test tubes with stoppers and label them as A1, A2, and A3. With a clean Morh's pipette, pipette 5mL of the 0.04\% starch solution into each of the test tubes A1, A2, and A3. Make sure your Morh's pipette is clean.        
                 \newstep[] Obtain about 20 mL of the enzyme solution (this is the enzyme solution or the artificial saliva solution). Make sure you do not contaminate the bottle of reagent by always retrieving liquid out of the bottle into a beaker and never directly pipetting from the bottle. Clean and rinse a Mohr's pipette with the enzyme solution.
                        \newstep[] Pipette 3mL from test tube A1 into a clean cuvette. Using the plastic dropper, add one drop of the enzyme solution. Very quicly, take small parafilm square and pull tight over top of cuvette hold with thumb and invert once to mix. Start measuring the absorbances and start counting time. 
         \newstep[] Measure absorbance every 15 seconds for two minutes. Write down the results in the results section.
                        
         \newstep[] Repeat the previous two steps for test tubes  A2 and A3. Once done, clean the test tubes A2 and A3 and have them ready for reuse.
         
                        \newstep[] Using PH paper, measure the PH of any of the enzyme+starch mixtures. This PH value will be used as reference. Write down this PH in the results section.
         \newstep[] Process the data in the results section and obtain initial velocities for your three measurements. Make sure your measured initial velocities are reproducible. Do not proceed until your instructor assesses the validity of your results.

         \end{steps}

